<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Altava</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Altava was a Roman town in Mauretania Caesariensis, today Ouled Mimoun near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>. An inscription there dated 508 names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masuna">Masuna</a> as king of the peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, the main evidence for a Romano-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> kingdom in the region. It remained a centre of Berber rulers until the Arab conquest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/altava.jpg" alt="Altava" width="320" height="107" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NordNordWest · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karte_R%C3%B6mische_St%C3%A4dte_in_Nordafrika.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Altava stood in the west of Mauretania Caesariensis, on the Roman road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tebessa">Theveste</a> to Numerus Syrorum, the present-day Maghnia. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> it was a city mainly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> with a small Roman garrison, which the historian M. Ruiu identifies as the Cohors II Sardorum. The garrison guarded the new frontier, moved south from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> shore to a military road called the Nova Praetentura, which ran from Rapidum in Numidia to Altava and Numerus Syrorum on the border of Mauretania Tingitana.</p><p>According to the historian Lawless, Altava was a vicus that gained independent status from the garrison's fort. It had a large forum and an important pagan temple, later converted into a Christian church. The Roman settlement covered nearly 13 hectares and was surrounded by farms. Under French rule the town was called Lamoriciere.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the Crisis of the 3rd Century, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> reorganised the empire in 298 and later withdrew from the area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volubilis">Volubilis</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rif">Rif Mountains</a> and the western Algerian Atlas. Berber rulers created a small independent kingdom there, centred on Altava and the fully Romanised city of Volubilis. After the Vandal invasion of 429 Altava became the capital of an independent Berber state, at some distance from the Vandal heartland, which borrowed the social, military and religious structure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p><p>From the 7th century Byzantine historians usually called it the Kingdom of Altava. Between 550 and 578 Altava was the capital of the independent Christian Berber kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garmul">Garmul</a>. The kingdom is last mentioned in connection with the campaign of Gennadius, a magister militum of the Eastern Roman Empire, who defeated the Berbers and the ruler of Altava in 578. No records of Altava survive from the 7th century, though the kingdom, or at least part of it, may have lasted until the Arab conquest around 700.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Altava” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Gaiseric's death the kingdom lost ground to the Berbers: the Kingdom of <b>Altava</b>, Masties's kingdom of the Aurès in 483–484, Guenfan's Frexenses south of Thugga in 510 and Cabaon's Laguatan, who took Oea and Sabratha and defeated Thrasamund's expedition of 523.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vandal-kingdom">Vandal Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the death of Garmul of the Kingdom of the Moors and Romans in 578, Berber rulers continued to hold the region of <b>Altava</b> and the plains of western Algeria.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-altava">Kingdom of Altava</a></li><li><blockquote>An inscription at <b>Altava</b> (Ouled Mimoun) dated 508 names Masuna as 'king of the peoples of the Moors and Romans', the best evidence for this kingdom, which probably arose in the 470s or 480s.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-moors-and-romans">Kingdom of the Moors and Romans</a></li></ul></section>
